    idm: ['Slender body with a short, broad snout and triangular pectoral fins ^2,4', 'Two triangular dorsal fins ^3', 'Silver-gray to bronze back with dark saddle bars and spots; paler or white below ^2,3,4', 'Spots fade toward the center of each saddle as the shark ages ^4'],
    lk: [['Swell Shark', 'Reddish-brown with a flattened head. ^2']],
    sz: 'Adults are usually 4–5 ft; over 6 ft is rare. Up to roughly 40 lb. ^2,3,4',
    bait: 'Mostly caught on baited hooks. Its natural diet is crabs, ghost shrimp, clams, worms, octopus, and fish such as herring and anchovy. ^1',
    tech: ['Most angler catch comes from private boats and from shore. ^1', 'In northern California it follows the tide over shallow mudflats. ^1', 'It moves inshore to feed at high tide and drops back to deeper water at low tide. ^3,4'],
    where: ['Muddy or sandy bays, sloughs and estuaries; big populations in San Francisco Bay ^1,2', 'Southern California open coast: kelp beds, sand near rocky reefs, surf zone ^1', 'Mostly in water about 20 ft deep or less ^1,2'],
    when: ['Present year-round ^3', 'Spring and summer in bays and estuaries; often moves to coastal waters in winter ^4', 'Feeds on shallow flats as the tide comes in ^4'],
    tip: ['Poses almost no danger to people. ^2', 'Flesh is high in mercury; one serving a week at most, and check California health advisories. ^2,3', 'Gut, bleed and ice it quickly or the meat can smell and taste of ammonia. ^3', 'Slow-growing and late to mature, so overfishing hurts it. ^1,2'],
