    idm: ['Large, hefty dabbling duck with a rounded head and wide flat bill ^1', 'Breeding male: glossy green head, yellow bill, white neck ring, brown chest, gray body, black rear ^1,2', 'Female: mottled brown with an orange bill that has dark smudging ^1,2', 'Both sexes show a blue wing patch bordered in white ^1,2', 'Male has a black-and-white tail; nonbreeding males look browner like females but keep the tail and some green ^1', 'Voice: female gives the loud classic quack; male gives soft, reedy notes ^2'],
    lk: [['American Black Duck', 'Often hybridizes with Mallards; hybrid males are darker with only a partly green head. ^1,2'], ['Mottled Duck', 'Both sexes look like Mallards; found in Florida and the Gulf Coast. ^1,2'], ['Northern Shoveler', 'Similar breeding male and female/immature plumages. ^1']],
    sz: '20–26 in long, 32–37 in wingspan, about 2.2–2.9 lb. ^1,2',
    tech: ['Watch shallow water for ducks tipping tail-up to feed; Mallards dabble and rarely dive. ^1,2', 'Check for the white-bordered blue wing patch in flight. ^1,2', 'Late-summer birds in eclipse plumage look like females; check the tail and any green on the head. ^1'],
    where: ['Almost any wetland: lakes, ponds, marshes, rivers, coastal bays ^1,2', 'City and suburban parks, backyards, ditches, pastures and rice fields ^1,2', 'Prefers fresh water; uses sheltered bays and estuaries in winter ^2'],
    when: ['Pairs form in fall and winter, well before breeding ^1,2', 'Late summer: adults lose all flight feathers and cannot fly for 3-4 weeks ^1', 'Wild North American birds are mostly migratory; city park birds may stay all year ^2'],
    tip: ['Park birds are often tame and used to handouts; wild ones are wary and fast flyers ^1,2', 'Ducklings leave the nest within a day of hatching and follow the female ^1,2', 'One of the most abundant and widespread ducks in the world ^2'],
