    idm: ['Black head and neck with a white chinstrap patch ^1,2,3,4', 'Tan to gray-brown breast, brown back, white belly and undertail ^2,4', 'In flight, a white U shows over the dark tail ^2', 'Big waterbird: larger than a Mallard, smaller than a Mute Swan ^2', 'Voice: loud, flat honking. Flocks fly in V formations or lines ^1,2,3,4'],
    lk: [['Cackling Goose', 'The smallest forms of the group are now counted as a separate species. ^1,3']],
    sz: 'Length 30–43 in, wingspan 50–67 in, weight roughly 7–20 lb. Birds run smaller farther north and darker farther west. ^2',
    where: ['Lakes, rivers, ponds and other water of any size ^2', 'Park lawns, golf courses, athletic fields and farm fields, where they graze ^2,3,4', 'Marshes and bays. When inland water freezes they gather in coastal bays and inlets (Connecticut) ^3,4'],
    when: ['Many flocks now stay year-round in towns and suburbs ^1,2,3', 'Migrants fly north in spring and south in fall ^3', 'Connecticut: resident geese start nesting in early April. Migrants arrive early October, peak mid-December, mostly gone by mid-January ^4', 'Some birds move north in late summer to molt ^1'],
    tip: ['Alert birds stand with the neck stretched straight up ^2', 'Can be aggressive when threatened. Males defend territory by hissing and head-pumping ^2,3', 'Mates often stay together for life ^3,4', 'Connecticut\'s wildlife agency strongly discourages feeding geese. Bread is poor food and feeding makes a mess ^4', 'The USFWS manages them as migratory game birds ^4'],
    rng: 'Across most of North America, from Arctic tundra nesting grounds to city parks in the lower 48. Many now live year-round in one area. ^1,2,3',
